### 1. Introduction

Git is a distributed version-control system used to record and manage changes in source code. GitHub and GitLab are online platforms that host Git repositories and provide collaboration features. Visual Studio Code (VS Code) is a source-code editor with built-in Git support. These tools are commonly used together in modern software development. 

### 2. Questions and Answers

Q1. What is Git?
Git is a distributed version-control system. It helps developers track changes in files, maintain different versions of a project, create branches, and collaborate safely.

Q2. Why is Git used?
Git is used to keep a history of project changes, restore earlier versions, work on features independently, and collaborate with other developers.

Q3. What is a Git repository?
A Git repository is a project directory that contains the files and the Git information required to track their history and changes.

Q4. What is GitHub?
GitHub is an online platform for hosting Git repositories. It supports collaboration, code review, pull requests, issues, project management, and other development features.

Q5. What is GitLab?
GitLab is a Git-based development platform that provides repository hosting, collaboration, issue tracking, and DevOps features such as continuous integration and continuous delivery.

Q6. What is VS Code?
Visual Studio Code is a lightweight source-code editor developed by Microsoft. It supports many programming languages and provides an integrated terminal and Source Control features.

Q7. What is the difference between Git and GitHub?
Git is the version-control software that runs on a computer. GitHub is an online service used to host Git repositories and collaborate with others.

Q8. What is the difference between GitHub and GitLab?
Both are platforms for hosting Git repositories and collaborating on software projects. GitLab also has a strong focus on integrated DevOps and CI/CD workflows.

Q9. What is a commit?*
A commit is a saved snapshot of changes in a Git repository. A commit normally includes a message describing what was changed. 

Q10. What is a branch?
A branch is an independent line of development. Developers can create branches to work on features or fixes without immediately changing the main branch.

Q11. What is staging in Git?
Staging means selecting changes that will be included in the next commit. The command `git add` is commonly used to stage changes.

Q12. What is a remote repository?
A remote repository is a Git repository stored somewhere outside the local computer, such as on GitHub or GitLab.

Q13. What does `git clone` do?
It creates a local copy of an existing remote repository and downloads its files and Git history.

Q14. What does `git push` do?
It sends local commits to a remote repository such as GitHub or GitLab.

Q15. What does `git pull` do?
It downloads changes from a remote repository and integrates them into the current local branch. 

